<section class="why">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Why now</div>
    <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.9rem,3.6vw,2.8rem)">A signed policy is no<br>longer enough.</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1.4rem">Two changes land on Irish and UK employers this year. Both ask for training that is specific to the role, repeated, and recorded — the kind of thing an annual slide deck has never been able to prove.</p>
    <div class="deadlines">
      <div class="deadline">
        <div class="when">October 2026</div>
        <div class="where">United Kingdom &middot; Employment Rights Act 2025</div>
        <h3>All reasonable steps</h3>
        <p>The duty to prevent harassment rises from <em>reasonable</em> steps to <em>all</em> reasonable steps, and now covers customers, clients and contractors. Employers are expected to show scenario-based practice, a separate pathway for managers, role-specific content, and auditable records of who was trained and on what.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="deadline">
        <div class="when">August 2026</div>
        <div class="where">Ireland &amp; EU &middot; AI Act, Article 4</div>
        <h3>AI literacy, enforceable</h3>
        <p>Every employer using AI tools must give staff AI literacy proportionate to their role. Ireland's AI Office is now operational and inspections are documentation-led: a role inventory, a risk assessment, and training records tied to roles. There is no exemption for small companies, and one-size-fits-all training does not satisfy it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="feat">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-label">What we do</div>
    <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.9rem,3.6vw,2.8rem)">You hand us the subject.<br>We hand you the tutor.</h2>
    <div class="feat-grid">
      <div class="feat-item">
        <div class="n">01 — The interview</div>
        <h4>We take the knowledge out of your head</h4>
        <p>A structured questionnaire, run by us with your subject expert. Policies, procedures, the awkward edge cases, the things people always get wrong. That becomes the knowledge base your tutor teaches from — and it stays yours.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feat-item">
        <div class="n">02 — The tutor</div>
        <h4>A synthetic human, or one of your own people</h4>
        <p>Photorealistic and voice-accurate, fluent in 32 languages. If you would rather your staff were trained by your own Head of Compliance, we build the tutor from them instead — and they never have to run the session twice.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feat-item">
        <div class="n">03 — The conversation</div>
        <h4>Staff talk. The tutor answers.</h4>
        <p>Not a video and not a quiz. Your people ask questions out loud, in their own words, and work through real scenarios until they get them right. That is what practice looks like, and it is what the new rules ask for.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feat-item">
        <div class="n">04 — The evidence</div>
        <h4>Proof, not a completion tick</h4>
        <p>Who was trained, on what, when, and how they handled the scenarios — as a quarterly pack you can put in front of an auditor, a regulator or a tribunal. It also feeds into the LMS you already have.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

